    s_WHICH_BARS(py_str("Drop bars put your hands in three or four positions, which is what makes them comfortable on a long ride, and they drop your chest for less wind; flat bars give more control and a better view in traffic, and are the right answer for a town bike. If you ride for an hour or more on open roads, drops are worth the getting used to; if your rides are short or in traffic, flat bars with bar ends are the easier choice. Width matters as much as shape: about as wide as your shoulders, and a little wider for control off road."));
    s_MOVING_BARS(py_str("The height is set at the stem: undo the two bolts on the side and add or remove the spacers under it, which moves the bars 5 to 10mm at a time - tighten them back to just snug and check the steering turns freely. Too far away is a shorter stem, 10 or 20mm shorter, or a bar with less reach; too low for good is more spacers, and if the steerer has none left, a riser bar or a stem with a steeper angle does the same job. Numb or tingling hands is the bars too far or too low, or the saddle tipped nose-down, and it is worth fixing before a long ride rather than riding through."));
